WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.950
Did you have breakfast this morning?

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.808
Did you ever wonder after you eat breakfast and your food is digested,

00:00:09.858 --> 00:00:12.950
how is that glucose getting transported around your body?

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.546
Or as you take a breath in, how does that oxygen get transported around?

00:00:17.596 --> 00:00:21.950
Or when you exhale, how does that carbon dioxide get transferred out?

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.950
Are these only questions I ponder? Maybe.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.950
But your circulatory system is absolutely fascinating and highly involved in this.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.739
In this short intro video of the circulatory system,

00:00:33.789 --> 00:00:36.475
we will mention some basics about its functions and

00:00:36.525 --> 00:00:39.586
trace the pathway of how blood travels through your heart,

00:00:39.636 --> 00:00:41.624
but please know before we get started,

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:44.950
there are gigantic textbooks on the circulatory system itself.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.584
So, obviously, this video is just an intro. We’re going to first talk about blood:

00:00:50.634 --> 00:00:53.950
the medium of how we transport glucose and gases.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:01.950
As we mention in our body systems intro video from many years ago- there are some misconceptions.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.978
Human blood is red and always red although the shade of

00:01:05.028 --> 00:01:07.950
red can vary based on how much oxygen is in the blood.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.836
Veins and arteries are often drawn in diagrams as blue or

00:01:11.886 --> 00:01:16.677
red to show whether they have lower or higher concentrations of oxygen,

00:01:16.727 --> 00:01:19.950
but that’s just how it is used in most diagrams.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.950
It doesn’t mean the blood, or the veins, or arteries are actually that color.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.862
Veins that you see under your skin may look blue or green by the way,

00:01:28.912 --> 00:01:32.264
but that involves the way they appear under the skin and the

00:01:32.314 --> 00:01:35.950
reason for this would make a great physics topic — but I digress.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.468
Human blood has a lot of functions.

00:01:38.518 --> 00:01:42.497
It maintains a certain pH, temperature, osmotic pressure

00:01:42.547 --> 00:01:45.950
– all of this is very important for homeostasis.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.021
It transports things like hormones, nutrients, and gases.

00:01:50.071 --> 00:01:52.950
And it’s made up of different components.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.011
One component includes plasma, the liquid portion.

00:01:56.061 --> 00:02:01.950
Water, proteins, salts, lipids- you’ll find them in this liquid portion of blood known as plasma.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.514
Another component includes cellular components.

00:02:04.564 --> 00:02:07.950
This means red blood cells, which do the transporting of gases.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.950
White blood cells which can fight infections.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.109
And platelets, which are actually cellular fragments,

00:02:14.159 --> 00:02:17.089
and they’re involved with helping your blood clot.

00:02:17.139 --> 00:02:19.950
Very important when there is damage to the body.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.737
Red blood cells have an iron-containing protein called hemoglobin,

00:02:23.787 --> 00:02:26.950
and that is where that red coloring of blood comes from.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.747
So, when we’re talking about blood, and we’re just introing the circulatory system,

00:02:30.797 --> 00:02:33.950
we’re going to focus on how this blood moves around in the human body.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:40.742
Human heart anatomy observes the heart divided into two distinct and separated partitions;

00:02:40.792 --> 00:02:45.950
a deoxygenated, or low-oxygen partition, and an oxygenated partition.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.454
There are some human congenital heart conditions that

00:02:49.504 --> 00:02:53.950
can result in this oxygenated and deoxygenated blood mixing, however.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.579
More on that at the end. Arteries generally carry blood “away” from the heart.

00:02:59.629 --> 00:03:00.950
Think “A” for away.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.028
Arteries are typically oxygen-rich but there are exceptions.

00:03:05.078 --> 00:03:07.950
Veins generally carry blood “to” the heart.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.950
Veins typically are oxygen-poor but there are exceptions.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.507
Capillaries are small blood vessels and it is at the capillary level where oxygen is delivered to

00:03:17.557 --> 00:03:22.950
organs and tissues and where carbon dioxide will also be picked up to travel back to the lungs.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:27.592
So, looking at this heart, the right side (and that’s the person’s right,

00:03:27.642 --> 00:03:33.950
so for you it will look opposite) pumps deoxygenated blood and the left side pumps oxygenated blood.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.785
We can also see 4 chambers:

00:03:35.835 --> 00:03:40.950
the right atrium and right ventricle and the left atrium and left ventricle.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.370
I like to remember that A comes before V in the alphabet so that

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:47.950
helps me remember the A’s – for atria- are at the top of the heart.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.959
V for ventricles- are at the bottom of the heart.

00:03:51.009 --> 00:03:54.950
Atria also have thinner walls than the thicker walled ventricles.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.950
The heart also contains valves which we’ll see when we get to tracing the pathway of blood.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.246
The valves are one-way structures that help separate

00:04:03.296 --> 00:04:06.288
the chambers and also prevent backflow of blood.

00:04:06.338 --> 00:04:08.950
Ready to take the adventure of a lifetime?

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.602
An adventure tracing the pathway of blood through the heart?

00:04:12.652 --> 00:04:15.950
We’re going to start with blood that is in a human toe.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.950
This blood is deoxygenated.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.772
It needs to get to the heart so that it can be pumped

00:04:20.822 --> 00:04:24.500
to the lungs to pick up oxygen and then be spread throughout the body.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:26.950
It’s going to get there through the vena cava.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.767
Inferior vena cava to be specific as superior vena cava is above the heart.

00:04:31.817 --> 00:04:33.950
The blood enters the right atrium.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.603
The right atrium contracts,

00:04:35.653 --> 00:04:39.950
pushing the blood through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.750
The right ventricle contracts,

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:45.950
pumping the blood through the pulmonary valve to the pulmonary artery.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.950
By the way, when you see the word “pulmonary,” it likely involves the lungs.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.919
The pulmonary artery takes blood to the lungs where the red blood

00:04:54.969 --> 00:04:58.950
cells in the blood will take on oxygen and release carbon dioxide.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.779
Now this blood is oxygenated!

00:05:00.829 --> 00:05:05.950
It needs to return to the heart so that the heart can pump it throughout the body.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.950
The oxygenated blood travels through a pulmonary vein to the left atrium.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:14.408
The left atrium contracts and the blood travels through the mitral valve,

00:05:14.458 --> 00:05:17.950
also known as the bicuspid valve, into the left ventricle.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.602
The left ventricle contracts and pumps the blood through the

00:05:21.652 --> 00:05:24.950
aortic valve and out a major artery known as the aorta.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.950
The aorta is a major artery that carries oxygenated blood throughout the body.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.867
Now I don’t want to neglect the fact that the

00:05:32.917 --> 00:05:36.950
heart needs its own blood supply to deliver oxygen and glucose.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.321
The heart can receive this blood supply through coronary arteries.

00:05:41.371 --> 00:05:46.950
Coronary arteries branch off the aorta and eventually deliver blood into capillaries.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:50.950
These capillaries deliver oxygen and glucose to the heart.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.806
Coronary veins will take the deoxygenated blood to the right atrium

00:05:54.856 --> 00:05:58.950
where the blood will eventually travel the pathway to become oxygenated.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:07.315
In fact to quiz yourself, can you pause the video and trace the pathway of blood again?

00:06:07.365 --> 00:06:08.950
Ok, all together.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:15.950
Right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve, pulmonary artery, lungs,

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.461
back through the pulmonary vein, left atrium, mitral valve (bicuspid valve),

00:06:20.511 --> 00:06:22.183
left ventricle, aortic valve,

00:06:22.233 --> 00:06:25.388
aorta…takes it to the body and then it will eventually

00:06:25.438 --> 00:06:28.950
return through the vena cava back to the right atrium again.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:33.950
(phew) It almost makes you want to turn it into a song! But we won’t.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.478
The significance of the pathways, how they interact,

00:06:36.528 --> 00:06:38.033
the coordination of contraction,

00:06:38.083 --> 00:06:40.950
and many more elements are part of every beat of your heart.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:45.254
A human heart beats over 100,000 times per day so it’s significant

00:06:45.304 --> 00:06:49.950
that every beat is coordinated and blood is directed where it should go.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.980
The complexity of the cardiac cycle – which is the coordinated sequence of

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:58.772
the heart’s contractions and relaxations – isn’t something this short video can go into;

00:06:58.822 --> 00:07:00.950
hopefully a separate video on that soon.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:01.815
One last thing:

00:07:01.865 --> 00:07:05.969
there are many conditions in which the heart doesn’t function correctly.

00:07:06.019 --> 00:07:09.950
Anatomically, some heart conditions change the pathway flow of blood.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:13.976
One example that we had mentioned before is an atrial septal defect.

00:07:14.026 --> 00:07:19.169
The septum is the muscular wall that separates the right and left side of the heart. So,

00:07:19.219 --> 00:07:25.950
a septal defect could mean an opening and oxygen-rich blood could mix with oxygen-poor blood.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.855
Depending on the size, this can cause future problems such as an abnormal heartbeat,

00:07:30.905 --> 00:07:33.950
stroke, or potentially heart failure in severe cases.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:38.080
Some medications may help the symptoms or surgery can be an option.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:42.950
There continues to be more advancements for treating cardiovascular conditions.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:46.012
If you have interest in the amazing field of cardiology,

00:07:46.062 --> 00:07:49.950
take a look at the suggested further reading links in the video details!

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:55.950
Well, that’s it for the Amoeba Sisters, and we remind you to stay curious.
